        content:
          "<p>The itinerary includes a full acclimatization day at <strong>Namche Bazaar (3,438 m)</strong> and then follows a measured profile up the Dudh Koshi valley, gaining only two to three hundred metres of sleeping altitude a day between Dole, Machhermo, and Gokyo. That gradual approach is deliberate, because Gokyo itself sits at 4,800 m and the Gokyo Ri climb tops out at 5,357 m.</p><p>Gokyo Ri is done as a climb-high, sleep-low ascent — you gain over 500 m and return to the village to sleep, which is exactly the pattern that aids adaptation. Your guide checks the group each evening for headache, disturbed sleep, appetite loss, and breathlessness at rest. Drink three to four litres a day, walk slowly enough to talk comfortably, avoid alcohol above Namche, and report symptoms early. Descent resolves altitude illness quickly and reliably, and there is no shame in taking it.</p>",
